data.content:
Yes, C# fully supports `stackalloc` into a `Span<nint>` and `Span<nuint>`. Both work as expected:

```csharp
Span<nint>  span  = stackalloc nint[4];
Span<nuint> uspan = stackalloc nuint[3];
```

On this 64-bit system each element is 8 bytes (`nint.Size == 8`). On a 32-bit runtime they'd be 4 bytes. This has been supported since C# 7.2 introduced `Span`-based `stackalloc` and C# 9 added `nint`/`nuint` as first-class types.
